IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ucta-nouns`
IMPORT `ucta-logic`
IMPORT `ucta-schedules`
IMPORT `ucta-part3`
IMPORT `ucta-p1-explanatory`
IMPORT `ucta-p1-introductory`
IMPORT `ucta-p1-avoidance`
IMPORT `ucta-p1-goods`
IMPORT `ucta-p1-other`

-- Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation -- THE SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: a person who contracted on another party's written standard terms, or as a consumer, asking on facts
-- they can state whether a clause that excludes or limits liability is effective against them, and when it
-- must satisfy the reasonableness test.  This layer phrases the rules already encoded in the modules imported
-- above as the questions that person would ask.  Nothing here is new law, and no rule is restated here: each
-- question builds the modules' inputs from plain answers and calls the module's own function.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS.  Every question takes plain inputs: yes or no, a date, or a choice from a list.  A fact the
-- person may not know is asked as "Yes / No / I am not sure" (`My answer`); "I am not sure" becomes NOTHING,
-- and a rule that cannot answer without it answers NOTHING.  One answer differs: "has it been shown that the clause
-- is reasonable?" (section 11(5) puts that burden on the party who claims it), where "I am not sure" means "not
-- shown" and the answer is the same as for No.
--
-- THREE TIERS.  Whether the Act's limits reach the case at all (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule,
-- Part 3) is a question of its own, asked first for the section in question.  The section questions then take
-- the answer as one more input ("do the scope rules take my case out of this section?").  Part 3 is asked in
-- its own group at the end, in the text's order, and its answer is an input to the scope question.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does
-- not answer on these facts (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  Never read NOTHING as no.
-- The questions are worded so that JUST FALSE means "no": "Can the other party rely on the clause?" answered
-- JUST FALSE means the Act says it cannot.
--
-- A question is asked of the person who contracted as consumer or on standard terms.  There is no seat for an
-- official or a court: what a court must consider (ss 11(2), 11(4), 11(5)) is asked as a question of what the
-- person can expect.

DECLARE `My answer` IS ONE OF
    Yes
    No
    `I am not sure`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring: the person's answers become the modules' inputs.  Not questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An answer of Yes, No or "I am not sure" as a MAYBE BOOLEAN.
GIVEN answer IS A `My answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`what I say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN Yes THEN JUST TRUE -- input mapping
    WHEN No THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN `I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- Yes, No or "I am not sure" about the scope rules, as the modules' reach of a section.
GIVEN answer IS A `My answer`
GIVETH A `Reach of a section`
`what I say about the scope rules` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN Yes THEN `An exception takes the section out of the case` -- input mapping
    WHEN No THEN `No exception takes the section out of the case` -- input mapping
    WHEN `I am not sure` THEN `Whether an exception takes the section out of the case cannot be told` -- input mapping

-- The modules' reach of a section as an answer to "do the scope rules take my case out of the section?".
GIVEN reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`what the scope rules say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER reach
    WHEN `An exception takes the section out of the case` THEN JUST TRUE -- output mapping
    WHEN `No exception takes the section out of the case` THEN JUST FALSE -- output mapping
    WHEN `Whether an exception takes the section out of the case cannot be told` THEN NOTHING -- output mapping

-- The kind of loss from a yes or no.
GIVEN `it is death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Kind of loss`
`the loss I mean` MEANS
    IF `it is death or personal injury` THEN `Death or personal injury` ELSE `Other loss or damage` -- input mapping

§ `The Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 -- my questions`


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 1 and the First Schedule - does the Act reach my case?`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 1(1).  'Negligence' means a breach of a contractual duty of care or of a common law duty of care (not a stricter duty).
@export Is the breach negligence for the purposes of Part 1 (s 1(1))?
GIVEN `the breach is of a contractual duty to take reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the breach of an obligation, arising from the express or implied terms of a contract, to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill in the performance of the contract?
      `the breach is of a common law duty to take reasonable care and not a stricter duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the breach of a common law duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill (and not of any stricter duty)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the breach negligence` IS
    `s 1(1) the breach is negligence` `the breach is of a contractual duty to take reasonable care` `the breach is of a common law duty to take reasonable care and not a stricter duty`

-- s 1(3).  Sections 2 to 7 apply only to business liability; section 6 also reaches sale and hire-purchase (s 6(4)).
@export Is the liability business liability (s 1(3))?
GIVEN `the liability arises from things done in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the other party's liability arise from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether that person's own business or another's)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the liability business liability` IS
    `s 1(3) the liability is business liability` `the liability arises from things done in the course of a business`

-- s 1(4).  It is immaterial for this Part.
@export Does it make a difference that the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or that liability is direct or vicarious (s 1(4))?
GIVEN `the point asked about is whether the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or direct or vicarious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you asking whether it makes a difference that the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or that liability for it arises directly or vicariously?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does inadvertent or intentional make a difference` IS
    `s 1(4) it makes a difference for this Part` `the point asked about is whether the breach was inadvertent or intentional, or direct or vicarious`

-- s 1(2) and (3), First Schedule paras 1 to 4.  Yes means an exception takes the section out of your case; No means none does; not covered means it cannot be told.
@export Do the Act's scope rules take my case out of the section I am asking about (s 1(2) and (3), First Schedule)?
GIVEN `the section I am asking about` IS A `Section of Part 1` @desc Which section are you asking about: section 2(1), 2(2), 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7?
      `the liability arises from things done in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the other party's liability arise from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether that person's own business or another's)?
      `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `what the contract relates to` IS A `What the contract relates to` @desc What does the contract (or the part of it the clause is in) relate to: insurance, an interest in land, intellectual property, a company, securities, marine salvage or towage, a charterparty of a ship or hovercraft, the carriage of goods by ship or hovercraft, employment, or none of these?
      `goods are carried by ship or hovercraft under the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are goods carried by ship or hovercraft under the contract?
      `the contract specifies ship or hovercraft as the means of carriage over part of the journey` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract specify ship or hovercraft as the means of carriage over part of the journey?
      `the contract makes no provision as to the means of carriage and does not exclude ship or hovercraft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract make no provision as to the means of carriage and not exclude carriage by ship or hovercraft?
      `the clause operates for and in relation to the carriage of the goods by ship or hovercraft` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the clause operate for and in relation to the carriage of the goods by ship or hovercraft?
      `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `I am the employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the employee under a contract of employment?
      `Part 3 takes my case out of sections 2 to 7` IS A `My answer` @desc Does Part 3 of the Act (sections 26 to 30) take your case out of sections 2 to 7? Yes if it does, No if it does not, or say you are not sure; the Part 3 question below works it out.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the scope rules take my case out of the section` IS
    `what the scope rules say, as a fact` (`s 1(2) and (3) whether an exception takes the section out of the case` `the section I am asking about` (`Scope facts` OF (`is a yes` (`s 1(3) the liability is business liability` `the liability arises from things done in the course of a business`)), `the kind of contract`, `what the contract relates to`, `goods are carried by ship or hovercraft under the contract`, `the contract specifies ship or hovercraft as the means of carriage over part of the journey`, `the contract makes no provision as to the means of carriage and does not exclude ship or hovercraft`, `the clause operates for and in relation to the carriage of the goods by ship or hovercraft`, (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`), `I am the employee`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `Part 3 takes my case out of sections 2 to 7`))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 2 - negligence liability`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 2(1).  A person cannot exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence.
@export Can the other party rely on a clause that excludes or restricts its liability for my death or personal injury resulting from negligence (s 2(1))?
GIVEN `the loss is death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the loss death or personal injury (personal injury includes any disease and any impairment of physical or mental condition)? Answer No if it is other loss or damage.
      `the liability results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the liability result from negligence, that is a breach of a duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the other party exclude liability for death or injury from negligence` IS
    `s 2(1) the person can exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence` (`the loss I mean` `the loss is death or personal injury`) `the liability results from negligence` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 2(2).  For other loss or damage the bar lifts so far as the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.  JUST FALSE if a flat negative (the First Schedule, section 1(3), Part 3) takes the section out of your case.
@export Must a clause excluding or restricting liability for negligence for other loss or damage satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 2(2))?
GIVEN `the loss is death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the loss death or personal injury (personal injury includes any disease and any impairment of physical or mental condition)? Answer No if it is other loss or damage.
      `the liability results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the liability result from negligence, that is a breach of a duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the clause for other loss satisfy reasonableness under section 2` IS
    `s 2(2) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the term or notice` (`the loss I mean` `the loss is death or personal injury`) `the liability results from negligence` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 2(2).  JUST FALSE if the clause is not shown to be reasonable (it is for those claiming reasonableness to show it, s 11(5)); not covered if it is shown reasonable (a lifted bar grants nothing) or the section is out of the case.
@export Can the other party rely on a clause that excludes or restricts its liability for other loss or damage resulting from negligence (s 2(2))?
GIVEN `the loss is death or personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the loss death or personal injury (personal injury includes any disease and any impairment of physical or mental condition)? Answer No if it is other loss or damage.
      `the liability results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the liability result from negligence, that is a breach of a duty to take reasonable care or exercise reasonable skill?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Has it been shown that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made)? Yes if a court has found so or the other party has shown it. No if it has been found or shown not to. Or say you are not sure: section 11(5) puts the burden of showing that a term is reasonable on the party who claims it is, so Not sure is treated as not shown, and the answer is then the same as for No.
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the other party exclude liability for other loss from negligence` IS
    `s 2(2) the person can exclude or restrict liability for other loss or damage resulting from negligence` (`the loss I mean` `the loss is death or personal injury`) `the liability results from negligence` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 2(3).  Agreement to or awareness of the clause is not of itself voluntary acceptance of any risk.
@export Does my agreement to, or awareness of, a clause excluding liability for negligence mean I accepted the risk (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `the clause purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract term or notice purport to exclude or restrict liability for negligence?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my agreement to the clause mean I accepted the risk` IS
    `s 2(3) the person's agreement to or awareness of the term or notice is of itself to be taken as indicating voluntary acceptance of the risk` `the clause purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 2(3), in its own words.  JUST TRUE where the clause purports to exclude liability for negligence; JUST FALSE where it does not (the "Where" is not met) or the section is out of the case.
@export Does section 2(3) apply to my case, so that my agreement to, or awareness of, a clause excluding liability for negligence is not of itself taken as accepting the risk (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `the clause purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract term or notice purport to exclude or restrict liability for negligence?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 2(3) stop my agreement counting as accepting the risk` IS
    `s 2(3) the person's agreement to or awareness of the term or notice is not of itself to be taken as indicating voluntary acceptance of the risk` `the clause purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 3 - liability arising in contract`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 3(1).  It applies between contracting parties where one deals as consumer or on the other's written standard terms of business.
@export Does section 3 apply to my contract (s 3(1))?
GIVEN `the other party and I are parties to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you and the other party both parties to the contract?
      `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `I contracted on the other party's written standard terms of business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you contract on the other party's written standard terms of business?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 3 apply to my contract` IS
    `s 3(1) this section applies as between the contracting parties` `the other party and I are parties to the contract` (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `I contracted on the other party's written standard terms of business`

-- s 3(2).  The bar lifts so far as the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.  JUST FALSE if a flat negative (the First Schedule, section 1(3), Part 3) takes the section out of your case.
@export Must a clause that excludes the other party's liability for its own breach, or claims different or no performance, satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 3(2))?
GIVEN `section 3 applies to my contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Does section 3 apply to your contract (one of you deals as consumer or on the other's written standard terms of business)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the question on section 3(1) below works it out.
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `what the clause claims` IS A `What a term claims` @desc What does the clause claim: to exclude or restrict the other party's liability for its own breach, to allow it to give a performance substantially different from what you reasonably expected, to allow it to give no performance at all, or something else?
      `the other party is itself in breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the other party itself in breach of contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the clause satisfy reasonableness under section 3` IS
    `s 3(2) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` (`what I say, as a fact` `section 3 applies to my contract`) `what the other party relies on` `what the clause claims` `the other party is itself in breach of contract` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 3(2).  JUST FALSE if the clause is within (a) or (b) and is not shown to be reasonable (s 11(5)); not covered otherwise (a lifted bar grants nothing).
@export Can the other party rely on a clause that excludes its liability for its own breach, or claims to give a different or no performance (s 3(2))?
GIVEN `section 3 applies to my contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Does section 3 apply to your contract (one of you deals as consumer or on the other's written standard terms of business)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the question on section 3(1) below works it out.
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `what the clause claims` IS A `What a term claims` @desc What does the clause claim: to exclude or restrict the other party's liability for its own breach, to allow it to give a performance substantially different from what you reasonably expected, to allow it to give no performance at all, or something else?
      `the other party is itself in breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the other party itself in breach of contract?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Has it been shown that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made)? Yes if a court has found so or the other party has shown it. No if it has been found or shown not to. Or say you are not sure: section 11(5) puts the burden of showing that a term is reasonable on the party who claims it is, so Not sure is treated as not shown, and the answer is then the same as for No.
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the other party rely on the clause under section 3` IS
    `s 3(2) the other party can rely on the contract term as against that party` (`what I say, as a fact` `section 3 applies to my contract`) `what the other party relies on` `what the clause claims` `the other party is itself in breach of contract` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 4 - unreasonable indemnity clauses`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 4(1).  A person dealing as consumer cannot be made to indemnify another except so far as the contract term is reasonable.  JUST FALSE if a flat negative takes the section out of your case.
@export Must a clause making me indemnify another person satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 4(1))?
GIVEN `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `the clause makes me indemnify another person against that person's liability for negligence or breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the clause make you indemnify another person (a party to the contract or not) against liability that person may incur for negligence or breach of contract?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the indemnity clause satisfy reasonableness` IS
    `s 4(1) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `the clause makes me indemnify another person against that person's liability for negligence or breach of contract` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 4(1).  JUST FALSE if the term is not shown to be reasonable (s 11(5)); not covered if it is shown reasonable (a lifted bar grants nothing).
@export Can I be made to indemnify another person against that person's liability for negligence or breach of contract (s 4(1))?
GIVEN `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `the clause makes me indemnify another person against that person's liability for negligence or breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the clause make you indemnify another person (a party to the contract or not) against liability that person may incur for negligence or breach of contract?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Has it been shown that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made)? Yes if a court has found so or the other party has shown it. No if it has been found or shown not to. Or say you are not sure: section 11(5) puts the burden of showing that a term is reasonable on the party who claims it is, so Not sure is treated as not shown, and the answer is then the same as for No.
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be made to indemnify another person` IS
    `s 4(1) the person dealing as consumer can be made to indemnify the other person` (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `the clause makes me indemnify another person against that person's liability for negligence or breach of contract` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 4(2).  Section 4 applies whether the liability is the person's directly or vicariously, and whether it is owed to the consumer or someone else.
@export Does section 4 apply whether the liability is direct or vicarious, and whether it is owed to me or to someone else (s 4(2))?
GIVEN `how the liability is incurred` IS A `How the liability is incurred` @desc Is the liability the direct liability of the person to be indemnified, is it incurred by that person vicariously, or in another way?
      `to whom the liability is owed` IS A `To whom the liability is owed` @desc Is the liability owed to you as the person dealing as consumer, or to someone else?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 4 apply to this liability` IS
    `s 4(2) section 4 applies to the liability` `how the liability is incurred` `to whom the liability is owed`


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 5 - guarantee of consumer goods`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 5(2)(a).  Goods are in consumer use when a person is using them, or has them for use, otherwise than exclusively for the purposes of a business.
@export Are the goods in consumer use (s 5(2)(a))?
GIVEN `a person is using the goods or has them for use otherwise than exclusively for a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a person using the goods, or does a person have them in possession for use, otherwise than exclusively for the purposes of a business?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are the goods in consumer use` IS
    `s 5(2)(a) the goods are in consumer use` `a person is using the goods or has them for use otherwise than exclusively for a business`

-- s 5(2)(b).  Anything in writing is a guarantee if it contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good.
@export Is the document a guarantee (s 5(2)(b))?
GIVEN `the document is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the document in writing?
      `the document contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the document contain, or purport to contain, some promise or assurance (however worded or presented) that defects will be made good by complete or partial replacement, or by repair, monetary compensation or otherwise?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the document a guarantee` IS
    `s 5(2)(b) the writing is a guarantee` `the document is in writing` `the document contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good`

-- s 5(3).  The section does not apply between the parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed.
@export Does section 5 apply as between the other party and me (s 5(3))?
GIVEN `we are parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you and the other party the parties to a contract under or in pursuance of which possession or ownership of the goods passed?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 5 apply between us` IS
    `s 5(3) section 5 applies as between the parties` `we are parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed`

-- s 5(1).  Liability cannot be excluded by a term or notice in a guarantee of consumer goods that prove defective through negligence in manufacture or distribution.
@export Can liability for loss from defective consumer goods be excluded by a term or notice in a guarantee (s 5(1))?
GIVEN `the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption` IS A `My answer` @desc Are the goods of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
      `a person is using the goods or has them for use otherwise than exclusively for a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a person using the goods, or does a person have them in possession for use, otherwise than exclusively for the purposes of a business?
      `the loss arises from the goods proving defective` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the loss or damage arise from the goods proving defective?
      `the loss results from the negligence of a person concerned in making or distributing the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the loss or damage result from the negligence of a person concerned in the manufacture or distribution of the goods?
      `the document is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the document in writing?
      `the document contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the document contain, or purport to contain, some promise or assurance (however worded or presented) that defects will be made good by complete or partial replacement, or by repair, monetary compensation or otherwise?
      `we are parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you and the other party the parties to a contract under or in pursuance of which possession or ownership of the goods passed?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can liability be excluded by a guarantee` IS
    `s 5(1) the person can exclude or restrict liability for the loss or damage by a term or notice in a guarantee` (`what I say, as a fact` `the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption`) (`s 5(2)(a) the goods are in consumer use` `a person is using the goods or has them for use otherwise than exclusively for a business`) `the loss arises from the goods proving defective` `the loss results from the negligence of a person concerned in making or distributing the goods` (`s 5(2)(b) the writing is a guarantee` `the document is in writing` `the document contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good`) (`s 5(3) section 5 applies as between the parties` `we are parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 5(1), in its own words.  JUST TRUE where the loss arises from defective goods in consumer use through negligence in manufacture or distribution; JUST FALSE where the "where" is not met, section 5(3) disapplies the section or the section is out of the case; not covered where the goods are not shown to be of the private type or the term is not in a guarantee.
@export Does section 5(1) stop liability for loss from defective consumer goods being excluded by a term or notice in a guarantee (s 5(1))?
GIVEN `the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption` IS A `My answer` @desc Are the goods of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
      `a person is using the goods or has them for use otherwise than exclusively for a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a person using the goods, or does a person have them in possession for use, otherwise than exclusively for the purposes of a business?
      `the loss arises from the goods proving defective` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the loss or damage arise from the goods proving defective?
      `the loss results from the negligence of a person concerned in making or distributing the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the loss or damage result from the negligence of a person concerned in the manufacture or distribution of the goods?
      `the document is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the document in writing?
      `the document contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the document contain, or purport to contain, some promise or assurance (however worded or presented) that defects will be made good by complete or partial replacement, or by repair, monetary compensation or otherwise?
      `we are parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you and the other party the parties to a contract under or in pursuance of which possession or ownership of the goods passed?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 5 stop liability being excluded by a guarantee` IS
    `s 5(1) liability for the loss or damage cannot be excluded or restricted by a term or notice in a guarantee` (`what I say, as a fact` `the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption`) (`s 5(2)(a) the goods are in consumer use` `a person is using the goods or has them for use otherwise than exclusively for a business`) `the loss arises from the goods proving defective` `the loss results from the negligence of a person concerned in making or distributing the goods` (`s 5(2)(b) the writing is a guarantee` `the document is in writing` `the document contains or purports to contain a promise or assurance that defects will be made good`) (`s 5(3) section 5 applies as between the parties` `we are parties to a contract under which possession or ownership of the goods passed`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 6 - sale and hire-purchase`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 6(4).  The liabilities in section 6 include those under any contract of sale of goods or hire-purchase agreement.
@export Is the liability one that section 6 covers (s 6(4))?
GIVEN `the liability arises from things done in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the other party's liability arise from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether that person's own business or another's)?
      `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the liability one that section 6 covers` IS
    `s 6(4) the liability is one referred to in section 6` `the liability arises from things done in the course of a business` `the kind of contract`

-- s 6(1).  Liability under s 12 Sale of Goods Act 1979 or s 6 Hire-Purchase Act 1969 cannot be excluded by a contract term.
@export Can the other party exclude liability for breach of the title obligations in a sale or hire-purchase (s 6(1))?
GIVEN `the obligation the liability arises from` IS A `Obligation in a sale or hire-purchase` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: section 12 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, section 13, 14 or 15 of that Act, section 6 of the Hire-Purchase Act 1969, section 6A, 6B or 6C of that Act, or another obligation?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the seller exclude liability for title` IS
    `s 6(1) the person can exclude or restrict liability for breach of the obligations arising from the title provisions` `the obligation the liability arises from` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 6(2) and (3).  Not at all against a consumer; against a non-consumer, only so far as the term is shown to be reasonable (s 11(5)).
@export Can the other party exclude liability for breach of the quality and fitness obligations in a sale or hire-purchase (s 6(2) and (3))?
GIVEN `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `the obligation the liability arises from` IS A `Obligation in a sale or hire-purchase` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: section 12 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, section 13, 14 or 15 of that Act, section 6 of the Hire-Purchase Act 1969, section 6A, 6B or 6C of that Act, or another obligation?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Has it been shown that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made)? Yes if a court has found so or the other party has shown it. No if it has been found or shown not to. Or say you are not sure: section 11(5) puts the burden of showing that a term is reasonable on the party who claims it is, so Not sure is treated as not shown, and the answer is then the same as for No.
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the seller exclude liability for quality` IS
    `s 6(2) and (3) the person can exclude or restrict liability for breach of the obligations arising from the quality provisions` (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `the obligation the liability arises from` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 6(3).  Against a person dealing otherwise than as consumer, a term can be relied on only so far as it satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.  JUST FALSE if a flat negative takes the section out of your case.
@export Must a term excluding liability for the quality obligations satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 6(3))?
GIVEN `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `the obligation the liability arises from` IS A `Obligation in a sale or hire-purchase` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: section 12 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, section 13, 14 or 15 of that Act, section 6 of the Hire-Purchase Act 1969, section 6A, 6B or 6C of that Act, or another obligation?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the quality term satisfy reasonableness under section 6` IS
    `s 6(3) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `the obligation the liability arises from` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Section 7 - miscellaneous contracts under which goods pass`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 7(1).  Where possession or ownership of goods passes under a contract not governed by the law of sale of goods or hire-purchase, subsections (2) to (4) apply.
@export Do the rules in section 7(2) to (4) apply to my contract (s 7(1))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the rules in section 7 apply to my contract` IS
    `s 7(1) subsections (2) to (4) apply` `the kind of contract`

-- s 7(2) and (3).  Not at all against a consumer; against a non-consumer, only so far as the term is shown to be reasonable (s 11(5)).
@export Can the other party exclude liability for correspondence with description or sample, or for quality or fitness (s 7(2) and (3))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` IS A `Obligation in another goods contract` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: correspondence of the goods with description or sample, quality or fitness for a particular purpose, section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982, the right to transfer ownership or give possession, the assurance of quiet possession, or another obligation?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Has it been shown that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made)? Yes if a court has found so or the other party has shown it. No if it has been found or shown not to. Or say you are not sure: section 11(5) puts the burden of showing that a term is reasonable on the party who claims it is, so Not sure is treated as not shown, and the answer is then the same as for No.
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the supplier exclude liability for description or quality` IS
    `s 7(2) and (3) the person can exclude or restrict liability for the correspondence of the goods with description or sample, or their quality or fitness` (`s 7(1) subsections (2) to (4) apply` `the kind of contract`) (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 7(3).  Against a person dealing otherwise than as consumer, the term can be relied on only so far as it is reasonable.  JUST FALSE if a flat negative takes the section out of your case.
@export Must a term excluding liability for description, sample, quality or fitness satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 7(3))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `I deal as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc Do you deal as consumer (you do not make the contract in the course of a business or hold yourself out as doing so, the other party does make it in the course of a business, and for a contract about goods the goods are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the consumer question below works it out. Not sure is treated as not known, and you get an answer only if the Act gives the same answer whichever way it is settled (section 12(3) puts the burden of showing that you do not deal as consumer on the party who claims it, but the Act does not say what follows if nothing is shown).
      `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` IS A `Obligation in another goods contract` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: correspondence of the goods with description or sample, quality or fitness for a particular purpose, section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982, the right to transfer ownership or give possession, the assurance of quiet possession, or another obligation?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the term satisfy reasonableness under section 7(3)` IS
    `s 7(3) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` (`s 7(1) subsections (2) to (4) apply` `the kind of contract`) (`what I say, as a fact` `I deal as consumer`) `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 7(3A).  Liability under section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 cannot be excluded by such a term.
@export Can the other party exclude liability under section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 (s 7(3A))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` IS A `Obligation in another goods contract` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: correspondence of the goods with description or sample, quality or fitness for a particular purpose, section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982, the right to transfer ownership or give possession, the assurance of quiet possession, or another obligation?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the supplier exclude liability for title under the 1982 Act` IS
    `s 7(3A) the person can exclude or restrict liability for breach of the obligations arising under section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982` (`s 7(1) subsections (2) to (4) apply` `the kind of contract`) `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 7(4).  In a case to which subsection (3A) does not apply, the bar lifts so far as the term is reasonable.  JUST FALSE if a flat negative takes the section out of your case.
@export Must a term excluding liability for the right to transfer ownership or quiet possession satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 7(4))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` IS A `Obligation in another goods contract` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: correspondence of the goods with description or sample, quality or fitness for a particular purpose, section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982, the right to transfer ownership or give possession, the assurance of quiet possession, or another obligation?
      `section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 also applies to the liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the liability also arise under section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 (implied terms about title)?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the term for ownership or quiet possession satisfy reasonableness` IS
    `s 7(4) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` (`s 7(1) subsections (2) to (4) apply` `the kind of contract`) `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` `section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 also applies to the liability` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)

-- s 7(4).  JUST FALSE if the term is not shown to be reasonable (s 11(5)); not covered if it is shown reasonable (a lifted bar grants nothing).
@export Can the other party exclude liability for the right to transfer ownership or the assurance of quiet possession (s 7(4))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` IS A `Obligation in another goods contract` @desc Which obligation does the liability arise from: correspondence of the goods with description or sample, quality or fitness for a particular purpose, section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982, the right to transfer ownership or give possession, the assurance of quiet possession, or another obligation?
      `section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 also applies to the liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the liability also arise under section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 (implied terms about title)?
      `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Has it been shown that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made)? Yes if a court has found so or the other party has shown it. No if it has been found or shown not to. Or say you are not sure: section 11(5) puts the burden of showing that a term is reasonable on the party who claims it is, so Not sure is treated as not shown, and the answer is then the same as for No.
      `the scope rules take my case out of this section` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the Act's scope rules (section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule and Part 3) take your case out of this section? Yes if they do, No if they do not, or say you are not sure. The scope question below works it out from the facts. Yes means the Act's own words take your case out of the section (for example the First Schedule says sections 2 to 4 "do not extend to" a contract of insurance): a question about whether a requirement of reasonableness applies is then answered No. Not sure leaves the section's answer open (not covered).
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the supplier exclude liability for ownership or quiet possession` IS
    `s 7(4) the person can exclude or restrict liability for the right to transfer ownership or the assurance of quiet possession` (`s 7(1) subsections (2) to (4) apply` `the kind of contract`) `the obligation the liability arises from, in a contract under which goods pass` `section 2 of the Supply of Goods Act 1982 also applies to the liability` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`) (`what I say about the scope rules` `the scope rules take my case out of this section`)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Sections 9 and 10 - effect of breach; secondary contracts`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 9(1).  A term that has to satisfy reasonableness may be found to do so, notwithstanding termination.
@export If the contract has been terminated, can the clause still be found reasonable and given effect (s 9(1))?
GIVEN `for reliance upon it the clause has to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For the other party to rely on the clause, does the clause have to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness?
      `the contract has been terminated by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the contract been terminated, either by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the clause be given effect after termination` IS
    `s 9(1) the contract term may be found to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness and be given effect` `for reliance upon it the clause has to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` `the contract has been terminated by breach or by a party electing to treat it as repudiated`

-- s 9(2).  Affirming the contract does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness.
@export If the contract was affirmed after a breach, does that of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness (s 9(2))?
GIVEN `there was a breach of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was there a breach of the contract?
      `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the contract nevertheless been affirmed by a party who was entitled to treat it as repudiated?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does affirming the contract exclude reasonableness` IS
    `s 9(2) affirming the contract of itself excludes the requirement of reasonableness in relation to the contract term` `there was a breach of the contract` `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated`

-- s 9(2), in its own words.  JUST TRUE where there was a breach and the contract was affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated; JUST FALSE where the "Where" is not met.
@export If the contract was affirmed after a breach, does the requirement of reasonableness still apply to the clause (s 9(2))?
GIVEN `there was a breach of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was there a breach of the contract?
      `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the contract nevertheless been affirmed by a party who was entitled to treat it as repudiated?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does affirming the contract leave the requirement of reasonableness in place` IS
    `s 9(2) affirming the contract does not of itself exclude the requirement of reasonableness in relation to the contract term` `there was a breach of the contract` `the contract is nevertheless affirmed by a party entitled to treat it as repudiated`

-- s 10.  A person is not bound by a term taking away rights under another contract, so far as they extend to enforcing a liability this Part prevents being excluded.
@export Am I bound by a term in one contract that takes away my rights against another person under a different contract (s 10)?
GIVEN `the term prejudices or takes away rights I have under another contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the term prejudice or take away rights of yours which arise under, or in connection with the performance of, another contract?
      `those rights extend to the enforcement of another person's liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do those rights extend to the enforcement of another person's liability?
      `this Part prevents that other person from excluding or restricting that liability` IS A `My answer` @desc Does Part 1 of the Act prevent that other person from excluding or restricting that liability? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the questions on sections 2 to 7 above work it out.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I bound by the secondary contract term` IS
    `s 10 the person is bound by the contract term` `the term prejudices or takes away rights I have under another contract` `those rights extend to the enforcement of another person's liability` (`what I say, as a fact` `this Part prevents that other person from excluding or restricting that liability`)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Sections 11 to 14 - reasonableness, dealing as consumer, varieties of exemption clause, definitions`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- ss 11(1), 11(3), 29(2).  The judgment is the court's; section 29(2) deems it for a term approved by a competent authority.
@export Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness (s 11(1) and (3); s 29(2))?
GIVEN `what the other party relies on` IS A `What is relied on` @desc What is the other party relying on: a term of the contract, a notice that forms part of the contract, or a notice that does not form part of the contract?
      `a court has found the clause reasonable` IS A `My answer` @desc Has a court or arbitrator found that the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (for a contract term: that it was fair and reasonable to include it, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made; for a notice that is not part of the contract: that it is fair and reasonable to allow reliance on it, having regard to all the circumstances obtaining when the liability arose or would have arisen)? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
      `a competent authority approved or incorporated the term` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the question settled by section 29(2) (the term was incorporated or approved by a competent authority acting under a statutory jurisdiction or function, and the authority is not itself a party to the contract)? Yes if section 29(2) applies, No if not, or say you are not sure.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the clause satisfy the requirement of reasonableness` IS
    `ss 11(1), 11(3) and 29(2) the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` `what the other party relies on` (`what I say, as a fact` `a court has found the clause reasonable`) (`what I say, as a fact` `a competent authority approved or incorporated the term`)

-- s 11(2) and Second Schedule (a) to (e).
@export Which matters must the court consider in deciding reasonableness for section 6 or 7 (s 11(2), Second Schedule)?
GIVEN `the determination is for the purposes of section 6 or 7` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the reasonableness being decided for the purposes of section 6 or section 7?
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `Guideline`)
DECIDE `Which matters must the court consider for sections 6 and 7` IS
    `s 11(2) the matters to which regard is had in determining reasonableness under section 6 or 7` `the determination is for the purposes of section 6 or 7`

-- s 11(2).  It does not prevent the court holding, in accordance with a rule of law, that the term is not a term of the contract.
@export Does section 11(2) stop a court from holding that a term is not a term of the contract (s 11(2))?
GIVEN `the term purports to exclude or restrict a relevant liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the term purport to exclude or restrict a relevant liability?
      `the holding is in accordance with a rule of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the court or arbitrator be holding, in accordance with a rule of law, that the term is not a term of the contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 11 stop the court finding the term is not part of the contract` IS
    `s 11(2) the subsection prevents the court or arbitrator from holding that the term is not a term of the contract` `the term purports to exclude or restrict a relevant liability` `the holding is in accordance with a rule of law`

-- s 11(4).  The resources the party could expect to be available, and how far it could cover itself by insurance.
@export Which matters must the court consider where liability is capped at a sum of money (s 11(4))?
GIVEN `the other party seeks to restrict liability to a specified sum of money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the other party, by reference to a contract term or notice, seeking to restrict its liability to a specified sum of money?
      `the question arises whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the question arise whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness?
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `Matter for a capped term`)
DECIDE `Which matters must the court consider for a capped liability` IS
    `s 11(4) the matters to which regard is had where liability is restricted to a specified sum` `the other party seeks to restrict liability to a specified sum of money` `the question arises whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`

-- s 11(5).  It is for those claiming that a term or notice is reasonable to show that it is.
@export Who must show that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (s 11(5))?
GIVEN `the other party claims the clause is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the other party claiming that the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the other party show the clause is reasonable` IS
    `s 11(5) the party must show that the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` `the other party claims the clause is reasonable`

-- s 12(1) and (2).  Not in business and not holding out; the other party in business; for goods contracts, goods of a private type; never on a sale by auction or competitive tender.
@export Do I deal as consumer (s 12(1) and (2))?
GIVEN `I made the contract in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you make the contract in the course of a business?
      `I held myself out as making the contract in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you hold yourself out as making the contract in the course of a business?
      `the other party made the contract in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the other party make the contract in the course of a business?
      `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the goods passing are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption` IS A `My answer` @desc Are the goods passing under the contract of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption? Yes, No, or say you are not sure. (Only a contract of sale, hire-purchase, or one under which goods pass turns on this.)
      `I am the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I deal as consumer` IS
    `s 12(1) and (2) the party deals as consumer` (`Dealing facts` OF `I made the contract in the course of a business`, `I held myself out as making the contract in the course of a business`, `the other party made the contract in the course of a business`, `the kind of contract`, (`what I say, as a fact` `the goods passing are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption`), `I am the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender`)

-- s 12(3).  It is for those claiming that a party does not deal as consumer to show that he does not.
@export Must I show that the other party does not deal as consumer, or must it show that I do not (s 12(3))?
GIVEN `someone claims that I do not deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is someone claiming that you do not deal as consumer?
      `I am the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Who must show I do not deal as consumer` IS
    `s 12(3) the party must show that a party does not deal as consumer` `someone claims that I do not deal as consumer` `I am the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender`

-- s 13(1).  To the extent Part 1 prevents the exclusion of a liability, it also prevents conditions, limits on remedies and on evidence, and (for sections 2 and 5 to 7) excluding the relevant duty itself.
@export Can the other party use a restrictive condition, or a limit on my remedies, to avoid a liability the Act prevents it excluding (s 13(1))?
GIVEN `the variety of exemption clause` IS A `Variety of exemption clause` @desc What does the clause do: make the liability or its enforcement subject to restrictive or onerous conditions, restrict a right or remedy (or prejudice you for pursuing it), restrict rules of evidence or procedure, exclude or restrict the relevant obligation or duty itself, submit differences to arbitration, or another variety?
      `Part 1 prevents the exclusion or restriction of the liability` IS A `My answer` @desc Does Part 1 of the Act prevent the exclusion or restriction of the liability in question? Yes, No, or say you are not sure; the questions on sections 2 to 7 above work it out.
      `the section that prevents it` IS A `Section of Part 1` @desc Which section of Part 1 prevents the exclusion or restriction: section 2(1), 2(2), 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the other party avoid the liability by another kind of clause` IS
    `s 13(1) the person can use the exemption clause to avoid the liability` `the variety of exemption clause` (`what I say, as a fact` `Part 1 prevents the exclusion or restriction of the liability`) `the section that prevents it`

-- s 13(2).  An agreement in writing to submit differences to arbitration is not to be treated as excluding or restricting any liability.
@export Is an agreement to arbitrate treated as excluding or restricting liability (s 13(2))?
GIVEN `the variety of exemption clause` IS A `Variety of exemption clause` @desc What does the clause do: make the liability or its enforcement subject to restrictive or onerous conditions, restrict a right or remedy (or prejudice you for pursuing it), restrict rules of evidence or procedure, exclude or restrict the relevant obligation or duty itself, submit differences to arbitration, or another variety?
      `the agreement is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the agreement in writing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is an arbitration agreement an exemption clause` IS
    `s 13(2) the agreement is treated under this Part as excluding or restricting liability` `the variety of exemption clause` `the agreement is in writing`

-- s 14.  'Business' includes a profession and the activities of any Government department or local or public authority.
@export Is this activity a business for the purposes of the Act (s 14)?
GIVEN `the activity` IS A `Kind of activity` @desc What kind of activity is it: a profession, the activities of a Government department, of a local authority or of a public authority, or another activity?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this activity a business` IS
    `s 14 the activity is within "business"` `the activity`

-- s 14.  'Personal injury' includes any disease and any impairment of physical or mental condition.
@export Is this harm personal injury for the purposes of the Act (s 14)?
GIVEN `the harm` IS A `Kind of harm` @desc What kind of harm is it: death, a disease, an impairment of physical or mental condition, a bodily injury, or damage to property or another loss?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this harm personal injury` IS
    `s 14 the harm is within "personal injury"` `the harm`

-- s 14.  'Notice' includes an announcement, whether or not in writing, and any other communication or pretended communication.
@export Is this communication a notice for the purposes of the Act (s 14)?
GIVEN `the communication` IS A `Kind of communication` @desc What kind of communication is it: an announcement in writing, an announcement not in writing, another communication, a pretended communication, or something else?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this communication a notice` IS
    `s 14 the communication is within "notice"` `the communication`


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Part 3 - international supply contracts, choice of law, sea carriage, other legislation, transitional`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 26(3) and (4).  A contract of sale or under which goods pass, made by parties in different States, with a cross-border element.
@export Is my contract an international supply contract (s 26(3) and (4))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the parties have places of business in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you and the other party have your places of business (or, if you have none, your habitual residences) in the territories of different States?
      `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When the contract is concluded, are the goods in the course of carriage, or will they be carried, from the territory of one State to the territory of another?
      `the offer and acceptance were done in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the acts constituting the offer and the acceptance done in the territories of different States?
      `the contract provides for delivery to another State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract provide for delivery of the goods to the territory of a State other than the one in which the acts of offer and acceptance were done?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my contract an international supply contract` IS
    `s 26(3) and (4) the contract is an international supply contract` `the kind of contract` (`Part 3 facts` OF `the parties have places of business in different States`, `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another`, `the offer and acceptance were done in different States`, `the contract provides for delivery to another State`, FALSE, FALSE, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 26(1).  The limits imposed by Part 1 do not apply to liability under an international supply contract.
@export Do the Act's limits on excluding liability apply to my international supply contract (s 26(1))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the parties have places of business in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you and the other party have your places of business (or, if you have none, your habitual residences) in the territories of different States?
      `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When the contract is concluded, are the goods in the course of carriage, or will they be carried, from the territory of one State to the territory of another?
      `the offer and acceptance were done in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the acts constituting the offer and the acceptance done in the territories of different States?
      `the contract provides for delivery to another State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract provide for delivery of the goods to the territory of a State other than the one in which the acts of offer and acceptance were done?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the Act's limits apply to my international supply contract` IS
    `s 26(1) the limits imposed by this Act on excluding or restricting liability apply to liability under the contract` `the kind of contract` (`Part 3 facts` OF `the parties have places of business in different States`, `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another`, `the offer and acceptance were done in different States`, `the contract provides for delivery to another State`, FALSE, FALSE, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 26(2).  The terms of an international supply contract are not subject to a requirement of reasonableness under section 3 or 4.
@export Must the terms of my international supply contract satisfy the reasonableness requirement of section 3 or 4 (s 26(2))?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the parties have places of business in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you and the other party have your places of business (or, if you have none, your habitual residences) in the territories of different States?
      `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When the contract is concluded, are the goods in the course of carriage, or will they be carried, from the territory of one State to the territory of another?
      `the offer and acceptance were done in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the acts constituting the offer and the acceptance done in the territories of different States?
      `the contract provides for delivery to another State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract provide for delivery of the goods to the territory of a State other than the one in which the acts of offer and acceptance were done?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the terms of my international supply contract be reasonable` IS
    `s 26(2) the terms of the contract are subject to a requirement of reasonableness under section 3 or 4` `the kind of contract` (`Part 3 facts` OF `the parties have places of business in different States`, `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another`, `the offer and acceptance were done in different States`, `the contract provides for delivery to another State`, FALSE, FALSE, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 27(1).  Where Singapore law is the proper law only by choice of the parties, sections 2 to 7 do not operate as part of the proper law.
@export Do sections 2 to 7 apply to my contract if Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it (s 27(1))?
GIVEN `Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the proper law of the contract the law of Singapore only by choice of the parties (and apart from that choice would be the law of some country outside Singapore)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do sections 2 to 7 apply when Singapore law is only chosen` IS
    `s 27(1) sections 2 to 7 operate as part of the proper law` (`Part 3 facts` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, `Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it`, FALSE, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 27(1), in its own words.  JUST TRUE where Singapore law is the proper law only by choice of the parties; JUST FALSE where it is not (the "Where" is not met).
@export Do sections 2 to 7 stop operating as part of the proper law when Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it (s 27(1))?
GIVEN `Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the proper law of the contract the law of Singapore only by choice of the parties (and apart from that choice would be the law of some country outside Singapore)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do sections 2 to 7 stop operating when Singapore law is only chosen` IS
    `s 27(1) sections 2 to 7 do not operate as part of the proper law` (`Part 3 facts` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, `Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it`, FALSE, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 27(2).  The Act has effect despite a foreign-law term where it was imposed to evade the Act, or a consumer habitually resident in Singapore contracted on essential steps taken there.
@export Does the Act still protect me despite a term applying foreign law (s 27(2))?
GIVEN `the contract has a term applying the law of a country outside Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract have a term which applies, or purports to apply, the law of some country outside Singapore?
      `the term appears to the court to have been imposed to evade this Act` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the term appear to the court, arbitrator or arbiter to have been imposed wholly or mainly to enable the party imposing it to evade the Act? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
      `in the making of the contract one of the parties dealt as consumer` IS A `My answer` @desc In the making of the contract, did one of the parties deal as consumer? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
      `that party was then habitually resident in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that party then habitually resident in Singapore?
      `the essential steps necessary for the making of the contract were taken in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the essential steps necessary for the making of the contract taken in Singapore, whether by you or by others on your behalf?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act protect me despite a foreign law clause` IS
    `s 27(2) this Act has effect notwithstanding the term applying the law of a country outside Singapore` `the contract has a term applying the law of a country outside Singapore` (`what I say, as a fact` `the term appears to the court to have been imposed to evade this Act`) (`what I say, as a fact` `in the making of the contract one of the parties dealt as consumer`) `that party was then habitually resident in Singapore` `the essential steps necessary for the making of the contract were taken in Singapore`

-- s 28(1).  The section applies to a contract for carriage by sea of a passenger or of a passenger and luggage.
@export Does section 28 apply to my contract for carriage by sea of a passenger (s 28(1))?
GIVEN `the contract is for carriage by sea of a passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the carriage by sea of a passenger, or of a passenger and the passenger's luggage?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 28 apply to my sea carriage contract` IS
    `s 28(1) section 28 applies to the contract` (`Part 3 facts` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, `the contract is for carriage by sea of a passenger`, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 28(2).  A person is not precluded from excluding or restricting liability to the extent the Minister's order prescribes.
@export Does the Act stop the carrier excluding liability for my loss on a sea passage (s 28(2))?
GIVEN `the contract is for carriage by sea of a passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the carriage by sea of a passenger, or of a passenger and the passenger's luggage?
      `the Minister's order prescribes this exclusion` IS A `My answer` @desc Does an order made by the Minister prescribe this exclusion or restriction of liability, in these circumstances, to this extent, by this term? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act stop the sea carrier excluding liability` IS
    `s 28(2) this Act precludes the person from excluding or restricting liability for loss or damage` (`Part 3 facts` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, `the contract is for carriage by sea of a passenger`, (`what I say, as a fact` `the Minister's order prescribes this exclusion`), FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 29(1).  Nothing in the Act removes the effect of a provision authorised or required by an enactment, or made to comply with an international agreement.
@export Does the Act remove or restrict a contract provision that another enactment or an international agreement requires (s 29(1))?
GIVEN `the provision is authorised or required by an instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contractual provision authorised or required by the express terms or necessary implication of an instrument?
      `that instrument` IS A `Kind of instrument` @desc What is that instrument: legislation of Singapore, subsidiary legislation of Singapore, an instrument having effect by virtue of legislation of Singapore, or something else?
      `the provision was made with a view to compliance with an international agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the provision made with a view to compliance with an international agreement to which Singapore is a party?
      `the provision does not operate more restrictively than the agreement contemplates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the provision avoid operating more restrictively than the agreement contemplates?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act override a provision required by legislation or treaty` IS
    `s 29(1) this Act removes or restricts the effect of, or prevents reliance upon, the contractual provision` (`Part 3 facts` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, NOTHING, `the provision is authorised or required by an instrument`, `that instrument`, `the provision was made with a view to compliance with an international agreement`, `the provision does not operate more restrictively than the agreement contemplates`, (Date 1 1 2000), (Date 1 1 2000), NOTHING)

-- s 29(2).  A term incorporated or approved by a competent authority acting under a statutory jurisdiction or function is taken as reasonable, unless the authority is itself a party.
@export Is a term approved by a competent authority taken as reasonable (s 29(2))?
GIVEN `the term was incorporated or approved by a competent authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the term incorporated or approved by, or incorporated pursuant to a decision or ruling of, a competent authority?
      `the competent authority is` IS A `Kind of body` @desc What is the body: a court, an arbitrator or arbiter, a Government department, a public authority, or another body?
      `the jurisdiction or function is conferred by` IS A `Kind of instrument` @desc What confers the body's jurisdiction or function: legislation of Singapore, subsidiary legislation of Singapore, an instrument having effect by virtue of legislation of Singapore, or something else?
      `the competent authority is itself a party to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the competent authority itself a party to the contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is a term approved by an authority taken as reasonable` IS
    `s 29(2) the contract term is taken as satisfying the requirement of reasonableness` `the term was incorporated or approved by a competent authority` `the competent authority is` `the jurisdiction or function is conferred by` `the competent authority is itself a party to the contract`

-- s 30(2).  Nothing in the Act applies to a contract made or a loss suffered before 12 November 1993, except so far as it applied by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909.
@export Does the Act apply to a contract made, or a loss suffered, before 12 November 1993 (s 30(2))?
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the contract made?
      `the date the loss or damage was suffered` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the loss or damage suffered?
      `it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909` IS A `My answer` @desc Was the Act applicable to the contract or liability by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 as then in force? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act apply to a contract or loss before 12 November 1993` IS
    `s 30(2) this Act applies in relation to the contract or the liability for loss or damage` (`Part 3 facts` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, NOTHING, FALSE, `Something else`, FALSE, FALSE, `the date the contract was made`, `the date the loss or damage was suffered`, (`what I say, as a fact` `it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909`))

-- s 1(2) with ss 26(1), 26(2), 27(1), 28(2), 29(1) and 30(2).  Yes means an exception takes the case out; No means none does; not covered means it cannot be told.
@export Does Part 3 take my case out of sections 2 to 7 (s 1(2); ss 26 to 30)?
GIVEN `the kind of contract` IS A `Kind of contract` @desc Is the contract a contract of sale of goods, a hire-purchase agreement, another contract under which possession or ownership of goods passes, or another kind of contract?
      `the parties have places of business in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you and the other party have your places of business (or, if you have none, your habitual residences) in the territories of different States?
      `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When the contract is concluded, are the goods in the course of carriage, or will they be carried, from the territory of one State to the territory of another?
      `the offer and acceptance were done in different States` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the acts constituting the offer and the acceptance done in the territories of different States?
      `the contract provides for delivery to another State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract provide for delivery of the goods to the territory of a State other than the one in which the acts of offer and acceptance were done?
      `Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the proper law of the contract the law of Singapore only by choice of the parties (and apart from that choice would be the law of some country outside Singapore)?
      `the contract is for carriage by sea of a passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the carriage by sea of a passenger, or of a passenger and the passenger's luggage?
      `the Minister's order prescribes this exclusion` IS A `My answer` @desc Does an order made by the Minister prescribe this exclusion or restriction of liability, in these circumstances, to this extent, by this term? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
      `the provision is authorised or required by an instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contractual provision authorised or required by the express terms or necessary implication of an instrument?
      `that instrument` IS A `Kind of instrument` @desc What is that instrument: legislation of Singapore, subsidiary legislation of Singapore, an instrument having effect by virtue of legislation of Singapore, or something else?
      `the provision was made with a view to compliance with an international agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the provision made with a view to compliance with an international agreement to which Singapore is a party?
      `the provision does not operate more restrictively than the agreement contemplates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the provision avoid operating more restrictively than the agreement contemplates?
      `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the contract made?
      `the date the loss or damage was suffered` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the loss or damage suffered?
      `it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909` IS A `My answer` @desc Was the Act applicable to the contract or liability by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 as then in force? Yes, No, or say you are not sure.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does Part 3 take my case out of sections 2 to 7` IS
    `what the scope rules say, as a fact` (`s 1(2) whether Part 3 takes sections 2 to 7 out of the case` `the kind of contract` (`Part 3 facts` OF `the parties have places of business in different States`, `the goods are in the course of carriage or will be carried from one State to another`, `the offer and acceptance were done in different States`, `the contract provides for delivery to another State`, `Singapore law applies only because the parties chose it`, `the contract is for carriage by sea of a passenger`, (`what I say, as a fact` `the Minister's order prescribes this exclusion`), `the provision is authorised or required by an instrument`, `that instrument`, `the provision was made with a view to compliance with an international agreement`, `the provision does not operate more restrictively than the agreement contemplates`, `the date the contract was made`, `the date the loss or damage was suffered`, (`what I say, as a fact` `it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909`)))
